      { "$p69Comment": "P69 (lane cs): `__declspec` IS NO LONGER ERASED. Until this row changed it was a function-like macro expanding to NOTHING, so every modifier written in it vanished before the parser saw it: `__declspec(align(32))` gave an under-aligned object and `__declspec(thread)` one object shared by every thread, both in silence, where cl gives 32 and a per-thread object. The word is now a KEYWORD (`DeclspecKeyword`, the second spelling of `attrSpec`) and this row keeps only what a program can still ASK about it: on the formats whose GNU-family toolchain has it, mingw-w64 gcc DEFINES `__declspec` as a macro (measured: `#ifdef __declspec` is true there and false on cl, where it is a keyword), and this compiler presents that identity (`__GNUC__`, `__MINGW32__`), so headers that test it — tcl.h's `defined(__GNUC__) && defined(__declspec)` — must keep reading it as defined. OBJECT-LIKE AND EXPANDING TO ITSELF: the name is not replaced again inside its own replacement (C 6.10.5.4), so the keyword reaches the parser with whatever follows it untouched — one modifier, several separated by white space or commas (cl's forms), or none. A program's own `#define __declspec(x)` still takes effect, as it does on both references.", "name": "__declspec_",          "kind": "constant", "value": "__declspec", "availableObjectFormats": ["pe"], "programRedefinition": "ordinary", "impliedSurface": { "kind": "claims-nothing", "reason": "compiler-extension", "note": "The GNU-on-Windows toolchain defines this name as a macro; here it expands to itself, the keyword that introduces a `__declspec( … )` attribute specifier. It selects no header and reaches no symbol." } },
